  "text": "Regarding factors, children whose caretakers had a lower education level, were 3.204 times more likely to have anemia. Children who had more than four siblings were 3.428 times more likely to have anemia. The education level and number of siblings all are indicators of social economic status. In most of the setting in Uganda, a lower education is associated with a lower income, while a high number of children (siblings) also is often associated with poor social economic status, since the limited resources available, are to be divided among the many children resulting in subpar feeding and care for each of the children; which results in undernutrition and anemia [24, 25].",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 83,
  "text": "Having HIV stage two increased odds of anemia by 2.059 times, while the odds were increased by 3.093 for those with stage three. This was in agreement with findings by [9] who reported that the risk of anemia increased as HIV-clinical stage increased [9]. A higher HIV stage increases risk of opportunistic infections as well as the possibility of bone marrow suppression which in turn increase the risk of anemia [4]. The same can explain why a history of admission in the preceding 6 months was associated with higher odds for having anemia. The odds were increased by 1.081 times for one admission, by 1.577 times for two admissions and by 4.065 times for three admissions in agreement with finding by [20] in Mozambique.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 84,
  "text": "Children who were severely wasted were 1.224times more likely to have anemia compared to those who had a normal nutrition status, while the odds of anemia were increased by 3.847 times among those who were severely stunted and by 3.003 times among those who had concurrent stunting and wasting. Stunting is an indicator of chronic malnutrition while wasting indicates acute malnutrition. With malnutrition, comes a micronutrient deficiency, resulting in an increased risk of anemia. This was in agreement with findings in India, where malnutrition was found to be an important risk factor of anemia [23], in Burkina Faso where results  a significant correlation between BMI and anemia [18], in Mozambique, where anemic children had a higher risk of undernutrition [21], in Ethiopia, where having undernutrition was an independent predictor of anemia in children undergoing ART [8] and at Mulago Hospital, where a substantial correlation was found between moderate anemia and height for age Z-score <2 [1].",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 76,
  "text": "Another possible contributor to the high prevalence could have been inadequate breastfeeding. The reduced duration of breastfeeding has been shown to increase risk of childhood infections, some of which increase the risk of anemia [15]. Also inadequate breastfeeding increases the risk of malnutrition [11], which in turn increases risk of anemia as explained in the previous paragraphs. A similar picture has been reported in Uganda by Nabunya et al. (2020), whose community-based cross-sectional study of 428 mothers of children aged 0-5 months in the informal sector in Kampala found that only 42.8% exclusively breastfed their infants, and who noted that 36% of Ugandan children below 6 months are not exclusively breastfed, which would leave a large proportion of children exposed to the infection- and malnutrition-related pathways to anemia described above [26].",
