  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 75,
  "text": "It is also important to note that, undernutrition is often associated with micronutrient deficiencies [13]. This can be affirmed by the fact that majority of the participates in the current study had microcytic anemia (Table 3), whose main etiology is Iron deficiency. Another indicator of low social economic status is the education level, where over 70% of the caretakers either never had a formal education or only had a primary education (Table 1). The low education level generally affects the financial status as well as the understandingin regard to the care and nutrition of children. The poor social economic status can also result in comorbidities like intestinal warms which could also results in recurrent blood loss and anemia as reported by [14] in Ethiopia.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 76,
  "text": "Another possible contributor to the high prevalence could have been inadequate breastfeeding. The reduced duration of breastfeeding has been shown to increase risk of childhood infections, some of which increase the risk of anemia [15]. Also inadequate breastfeeding increases the risk of malnutrition [11], which in turn increases risk of anemia as explained in the previous paragraphs. A similar picture has been reported in Uganda by Nabunya et al. (2020), whose community-based cross-sectional study of 428 mothers of children aged 0-5 months in the informal sector in Kampala found that only 42.8% exclusively breastfed their infants, and who noted that 36% of Ugandan children below 6 months are not exclusively breastfed, which would leave a large proportion of children exposed to the infection- and malnutrition-related pathways to anemia described above [26].",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 77,
  "text": "The prevalence reported was comparable to that reported among the 164 children in Lagos, Nigeria, who were between the ages of 5 and 12 years 54.2% HIV-positive children were anemic [16], a study done in Mulago by Munyagwa et al which found that 50.7% of HIV positive children were anemic [5] as well as a study done in western Uganda at Mbarara Regional Referral Hospital where anemia was present in 57.6% of the HIV positive children [6]. The similarities can be explained by the comparability of the study populations (same sex, same age group) and study design (cross-sectional). However, unlike those studies, the current research in Kayunga provides additional insight into the burden of iron deficiency anemia; an aspect previously underexplored in Ugandan pediatric HIV populations. This is particularly relevant given Uganda\u2019s National Anaemia Policy (2002), which recommends routine screening and preventive treatment for anemia in HIV-positive children. Despite these guidelines, implementation remains inconsistent across regional hospitals, especially in semi-rural districts like Kayunga. The high prevalence of anemia (57.3%) and iron deficiency anemia (54.1% among anemic children) observed suggests gaps in operationalizing these national recommendations.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

